Moth gives every bounded numeric value an exact width or precision. The numeric
inventory has eleven fixed types, plus the separate exact `Dec` family and the
non-arithmetic `Byte` type. No project profile selects a different semantic
identity.

### Exact numeric inventory

- Signed integers: `I8`, `I16`, `I32`, `I64`
- Unsigned integers: `U8`, `U16`, `U32`, `U64`
- Binary floats: `F16`, `F32`, `F64`

Each integer name fixes its complete value range:

[table:
    [th: [: Type] [: Range]]
    [td: [: I8] [: -128 through 127]]
    [td: [: I16] [: -32768 through 32767]]
    [td: [: I32] [: -2147483648 through 2147483647]]
    [td: [: I64] [: -9223372036854775808 through 9223372036854775807]]
    [td: [: U8] [: 0 through 255]]
    [td: [: U16] [: 0 through 65535]]
    [td: [: U32] [: 0 through 4294967295]]
    [td: [: U64] [: 0 through 18446744073709551615]]
]

`F16`, `F32` and `F64` use IEEE-754 binary16, binary32 and binary64
precision. Binary floats keep subnormals and signed zero, but Moth values never
include `NaN` or either infinity. A conversion or operation that produces a
non-finite result fails at its selected precision.

`Bool` and `Char` remain non-numeric types.

### Defaults and numeric families

An unconstrained integer-origin value defaults to `I32`. An unconstrained
binary-float-origin value defaults to `F64`. Moth has no unsigned literal
category and no value-size-based type selection. An unconstrained whole literal
outside the `I32` range produces a diagnostic rather than retrying as `I64`,
`U64` or `Dec`. An independent exact `U64` receiver can materialise the full
`U64` range directly from the retained spelling.

The default pair chooses otherwise unconstrained origins. It does not impose a
minimum width on arithmetic. When inference selects `I8`, `U16` or `F16`, an
eligible same-type operation keeps that type and checks or rounds there. See
@./#operators (Operators).

The source grammar has no numeric suffixes or radix forms. Whole spelling
introduces an integer origin. Decimal-point or exponent spelling introduces a
binary-float origin. Delayed inference can choose a width or precision within
that family, but it does not turn a bound integer into a float or a bound
binary float into `Dec`. A mixed collection of raw whole and decimal/exponent
literals selects the binary-float family regardless of item order. A named
integer value does not change family to make such a collection fit.

Receiving compatibility remains exact outside the operator rules. An operator
may select a common arithmetic domain, but that does not grant general
assignment, parameter or return widening. Fixed integers and fixed binary
floats also require an explicit conversion to mix.

### `Byte`

`Byte` names one octet and remains distinct from `U8`, `I8` and `Char`.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    raw Byte = 255
]

- A whole literal from `0` through `255` can initialise `Byte`; a negative value cannot.
- `Byte` supports equality and unsigned-octet ordering with another `Byte`.
- `Byte` has no arithmetic operators, unary minus or range-loop domain.
- `Byte` and `U8` convert infallibly in both directions. Other numeric conversions compose through `U8`.
- `{Byte}` remains an ordinary collection, not a buffer source type or text encoding.

### `Dec` and `DecN`

`Dec` and `Dec0` name the same scale-zero arbitrary-precision integer.
`Dec1` through `Dec256` represent `coefficient / 10^scale`. Scale belongs to
the type identity, so `Dec2` and `Dec4` remain different types. `Dec01` and
scales above `256` are invalid.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    large Dec = 1000000000000000000000
    money Dec2 = 12.34
]

- Whole literals materialise exactly at any scale.
- Decimal-point and exponent literals must fit the receiving scale exactly. `money Dec2 = 1.239` fails rather than rounding.
- Integer conversion and scale widening preserve the value exactly. Scale narrowing and `Dec`-to-integer conversion fail if removed digits are non-zero or the target range cannot hold the value.
- `Dec` arithmetic follows its existing scale and per-operation rounding rules. Binary floats and `Byte` never mix with `Dec` implicitly.
- `Dec` exponentiation takes an `I32` exponent. It keeps the exponent separate from the base scale and preserves the existing negative-exponent failure.
- Canonical text trims fractional trailing zeroes, uses a leading zero before fractions and never uses exponent notation or negative zero.

`Dec` remains an immutable value, not a source-visible `BigInt` or an added
numeric trait family. See @./#numeric-literals (Numeric literals) and
@./#operators (Operators) for receiving and operation details.

### MON data boundary

MON remains schema-directed literal data, not a source-body inference engine.
Its parser keeps integer and decimal/exponent categories lossless until the
schema selects an exact destination. Rust `Integer` and `Decimal` data families
do not add Moth source types. See @../mon/#mon-format (MON format).

The Progress matrix owns current compiler and backend support:
@../progress (Progress).
